    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The debate between originalism and living constitutionalism regarding the Second Amendment centers on whether the \"right to keep and bear Arms\" grants an individual right specific to the text's original 1791 meaning or if it adapts over time to reflect evolving societal values. Proponents of originalism, such as those in the Supreme Court's Major Cases with judges like Chief Justice Roberts, argue for a strict historical reading that ensures the right remains constant regardless of modern military technology or public opinion. Conversely, advocates of living constitutionalism suggest that judicial interpretation should consider contemporary needs and context, allowing the scope of the right to expand or contract as public understanding of safety and self-defense evolves. This fundamental disagreement continues to shape judicial reasoning and litigation outcomes across multiple lower courts and in high-profile rulings like *Bruen*.",
      "source": "target",
